    "text": "<p>In amplitude modulation, the message signal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> volts and carrier signal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mfenced><mi>t</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> volts.&nbsp;The modulated signal now contains the message signal with lower side band and upper side band frequency, therefore the bandwidth of modulated signal is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kHz</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math> is:</p>",
